        pages: [
          {
            title: "Shlla'at",
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Shlla'at is a Sudanese fishing card game in which players capture cards from the table and from opponents' capture piles. A turn continues with a new draw after each capture until no capture is made. The Arabic name means 'uprooted'.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Country/Region of Origin: Sudan\nTime Period: Early 21st century\nHistorical Development: May have originated in Sudan; very popular there in early years of the 21st century. Fishing game with captures from table and opponents' piles.\nCultural Significance: Usually played by children, also enjoyed by adults; popular social and family game. Arabic name shlla'at (شَلَّعَت) means 'uprooted'.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Varies by player count\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise\n--',
